# Release & systemd smoke test (learned 2026-08-22)

## Incident

v0.1.8 shipped with CLI subcommands (`backup auto`) but many installs still had
user units calling the removed flag (`--backup auto`). Timer fired nightly;
service exited in ~24ms; no backups ran for days. Unit tests and Greptile 5/5
did not catch it — only the production path failed.

## When changing CLI, systemd units, or install/RPM scripts

Touch **every consumer**, not just source that writes new units:

1. `src/systemd.rs` — `ExecStart` must match clap subcommands in `src/main.rs`
2. `~/.config/systemd/user/backup-tool.service` on upgrade — add RPM `%post`
   migration or repair command; existing user files are not rewritten by binary-only upgrades
3. `README.md` — CLI examples must match shipped syntax
4. Integration test — assert generated service unit string invokes a valid CLI

## Human smoke test before every release (Norm runs this)

```bash
grep ExecStart ~/.config/systemd/user/backup-tool.service
/usr/bin/backup-tool backup auto          # must match ExecStart
systemctl --user start backup-tool.service
journalctl --user -u backup-tool.service -n 20 --no-pager
```

Pass = journal shows rsync/backup progress, not CLI parse errors.

## Agent duty on Backup-Tool releases

- Never claim the systemd unit is correct without reading the user's
  `~/.config/systemd/user/backup-tool.service` or verifying migration in spec.
- After CLI/interface changes, grep repo for old syntax (`--backup`, old unit names).
- Treat `cargo test` + Greptile as necessary but **not sufficient** for release sign-off.
